Task 1: Annotate Namespaces for Sidecar Proxy Injection
Subtask 1.1: Understand Sidecar Injection Concepts
Before implementing sidecar injection, let's understand what happens when we enable it:

Sidecar Proxy Injection automatically adds an Envoy proxy container to every pod in a namespace. This proxy:

Intercepts all network traffic to and from the application container
Provides traffic management, security, and observability features
Enables service mesh functionality without modifying application code
Subtask 1.2: Create a Test Namespace
First, let's create a dedicated namespace for our testing:

# Create a new namespace for testing sidecar injection
oc create namespace sidecar-test

# Verify the namespace was created
oc get namespaces | grep sidecar-test
Subtask 1.3: Enable Automatic Sidecar Injection
Now we'll annotate the namespace to enable automatic sidecar injection:

# Add the sidecar injection annotation to the namespace
oc label namespace sidecar-test istio-injection=enabled

# Verify the label was applied correctly
oc get namespace sidecar-test --show-labels
Expected Output:

NAME           STATUS   AGE   LABELS
sidecar-test   Active   2m    istio-injection=enabled
Subtask 1.4: Create Additional Test Namespaces
Let's create another namespace without sidecar injection for comparison:

# Create a namespace without sidecar injection
oc create namespace no-sidecar-test

# Verify both namespaces exist
oc get namespaces | grep -E "(sidecar-test|no-sidecar-test)"
Subtask 1.5: Verify Istio Configuration
Check that Istio is properly configured in your cluster:

# Check Istio system pods
oc get pods -n istio-system

# Verify the mutating admission webhook is running
oc get mutatingwebhookconfiguration | grep istio
Task 2: Verify Envoy Proxy Injection in Pods
Subtask 2.1: Deploy a Test Application
Let's deploy a simple application to test sidecar injection:

# Create a deployment file for our test application
cat << 'EOF' > test-app.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: test-app
  namespace: sidecar-test
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: test-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: test-app
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: test-app-service
  namespace: sidecar-test
spec:
  selector:
    app: test-app
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
  type: ClusterIP
EOF

# Apply the deployment
oc apply -f test-app.yaml
Subtask 2.2: Deploy the Same Application Without Sidecar Injection
# Create the same application in the no-sidecar namespace
cat << 'EOF' > test-app-no-sidecar.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: test-app-no-sidecar
  namespace: no-sidecar-test
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test-app-no-sidecar
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: test-app-no-sidecar
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: test-app
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
EOF

# Apply the deployment
oc apply -f test-app-no-sidecar.yaml
Subtask 2.3: Compare Pod Configurations
Now let's examine the differences between pods with and without sidecar injection:

# Check pods in the sidecar-enabled namespace
echo "=== Pods with Sidecar Injection ==="
oc get pods -n sidecar-test

# Check pods in the regular namespace
echo "=== Pods without Sidecar Injection ==="
oc get pods -n no-sidecar-test
Subtask 2.4: Examine Container Details
Let's look at the detailed container information:

# Get detailed information about the pod with sidecar injection
POD_WITH_SIDECAR=$(oc get pods -n sidecar-test -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Pod with sidecar: $POD_WITH_SIDECAR"

# Check the number of containers in the pod
oc get pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
echo ""

# Get detailed information about the pod without sidecar injection
POD_WITHOUT_SIDECAR=$(oc get pods -n no-sidecar-test -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Pod without sidecar: $POD_WITHOUT_SIDECAR"

# Check the number of containers in this pod
oc get pod $POD_WITHOUT_SIDECAR -n no-sidecar-test -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
echo ""
Subtask 2.5: Verify Envoy Proxy Container
Let's specifically look for the Envoy proxy container:

# Describe the pod with sidecar injection to see all containers
oc describe pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test | grep -A 5 -B 5 "istio-proxy"

# Check if the Envoy proxy is running
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- ps aux | grep envoy
Subtask 2.6: Examine Sidecar Configuration
# View the istio-proxy container configuration
oc get pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -o yaml | grep -A 20 -B 5 "istio-proxy"

# Check the init containers (Istio uses init containers for network setup)
oc get pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -o jsonpath='{.spec.initContainers[*].name}'
echo ""
Task 3: Monitor Service Mesh Activity Using OpenShift CLI
Subtask 3.1: Check Envoy Proxy Status
Let's verify that the Envoy proxy is functioning correctly:

# Check Envoy admin interface (if accessible)
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/stats | head -20

# Check Envoy configuration
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/config_dump | head -50
Subtask 3.2: Monitor Network Traffic
# Check listening ports in the sidecar-enabled pod
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- netstat -tlnp

# Compare with the pod without sidecar
oc exec $POD_WITHOUT_SIDECAR -n no-sidecar-test -- netstat -tlnp
Subtask 3.3: Generate Test Traffic
Let's create some traffic to monitor:

# Create a test client pod in the sidecar-enabled namespace
cat << 'EOF' > test-client.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-client
  namespace: sidecar-test
spec:
  containers:
  - name: test-client
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ['sleep', '3600']
EOF

# Apply the client pod
oc apply -f test-client.yaml

# Wait for the pod to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=Ready pod/test-client -n sidecar-test --timeout=60s
Subtask 3.4: Test Service Communication
# Generate traffic from the client to the service
oc exec test-client -n sidecar-test -- curl -s http://test-app-service/

# Check the response (should be nginx default page)
oc exec test-client -n sidecar-test -- curl -s -I http://test-app-service/
Subtask 3.5: Monitor Envoy Access Logs
# Check Envoy access logs
oc logs $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy | tail -10

# Generate more traffic and monitor logs in real-time
oc logs $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -f &

# In another terminal or after stopping the log stream, generate traffic
for i in {1..5}; do
  oc exec test-client -n sidecar-test -- curl -s http://test-app-service/ > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i sent"
  sleep 1
done
Subtask 3.6: Check Service Mesh Metrics
# Check Envoy metrics
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/stats | grep http

# Check cluster information
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/clusters
Subtask 3.7: Verify Service Discovery
# Check what services Envoy knows about
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/config_dump | grep -A 5 -B 5 "test-app-service"

# List all endpoints Envoy is aware of
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/clusters | grep -E "(test-app|outbound)"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Sidecar Not Injected
If you don't see the istio-proxy container in your pods:

# Check if the namespace is properly labeled
oc get namespace sidecar-test --show-labels

# Verify Istio system is running
oc get pods -n istio-system

# Check the mutating webhook configuration
oc get mutatingwebhookconfiguration istio-sidecar-injector -o yaml
Issue 2: Pod Startup Issues
If pods are failing to start after enabling sidecar injection:

# Check pod events
oc describe pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test

# Check init container logs
oc logs $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-init

# Verify security context and permissions
oc get pod $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -o yaml | grep -A 10 securityContext
Issue 3: Network Connectivity Problems
If applications can't communicate after sidecar injection:

# Check iptables rules (created by istio-init)
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- iptables -t nat -L

# Verify Envoy is listening on expected ports
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- netstat -tlnp | grep envoy

# Check Envoy cluster health
oc exec $POD_WITH_SIDECAR -n sidecar-test -c istio-proxy -- curl -s localhost:15000/clusters | grep health_flags
